💬 I stopped before changing anything, because this request collides with an explicit rule in this repo and I think you'll want to pick the direction.

## What I found

`design.html` at the repo root **is** the design system, and CLAUDE.md marks it a read-only input. It draws exactly five screens, all operator desk:

| Line | Screen |
|---|---|
| 20 | Catalog / Categories |
| 53 | Operations / Today |
| 184 | Orders / Fulfilment queue |
| 240 | Orders / Order detail |
| 334 | Edit category (drawer) |

The rule is stated repeatedly and in several forms — *"the retailer screens **are** the design and changing the design's palette is not a decision to make from inside the code"*, *"re-colouring them would be inventing a surface neither design draws."* A generic "make it look modern" pass is the specific thing that arrangement exists to prevent.

It would also not be a small diff. **118 spec rows** across four files pin exact design values (`apps/admin` alone has 84), plus 123 tokens in `tokens.css` — ten of which carry recorded AA deviations with the measured ratio written at the declaration. A restyle breaks those rows, and the failures wouldn't tell you whether the new look is *wrong* or just *different*, because there'd be no authority left to check it against.

## The part you may not expect

The modernization you're probably picturing has largely already happened — and was verified in a real browser, not just asserted. The last ~20 commits are exactly this work: WCAG AA contrast corrected across all three palettes, every control floored to 44px under `any-pointer: coarse`, focus-managed modals on all three surfaces, `prefers-reduced-motion`, component consolidation, fluid full-bleed layout. Twenty-two browser runs, most recently landing at 0 findings.

So the design-conformance backlog is genuinely worked down. What's left is blocked on *judgement*, not effort: four of the five desk drill-downs (invoice, credit note, retailer tax, negotiated prices) aren't drawn by `design.html` at all, so re-laying them out means inventing an arrangement rather than transcribing one.

## One thing I checked and it came back negative

`plan.md` defers tokenising `@udaan/web-ui`'s `Button` into a `--control-*` set — which is the closest thing here to "modern design pattern" in the design-tokens sense — with the trigger *"worth doing when a **second** `@udaan/web-ui` control needs desk metrics."*

`desk.css` does now override a second control, `TextField`, so I checked whether that trigger had fired. It hasn't: those overrides are a focus ring and the `optional` chip's colour (`desk.css:787–818`) — colour and affordance, which is what a token overlay is *supposed* to do. `Button` is still the only control needing desk **metrics**, so the deferral stands. Worth reporting as a negative result rather than silently not mentioning it.

## Where I'd go

Tell me which and I'll start:

1. **Tokenise `Button`** — the real design-system improvement on the board. Replaces a four-property override behind a doubled selector with `--control-*` tokens, and lets `AdminShell.css`'s specificity escalation be reverted. Holds the phone's current values so `apps/web` doesn't move. Its own trigger says not yet, but it's your call to override.
2. **Lay out the four undrawn drill-downs** — real work, but needs you to decide the arrangement, since the design is silent. I can propose options against the shapes already proven here.
3. **You actually do want a new visual direction** — then the honest path is updating `design.html` first and re-transcribing, not editing components. Much bigger, and worth being deliberate about.
4. **Something specific bothers you** — name the screen and I'll render it and measure.

Which one? If there's a screen that prompted this, that's the most useful thing you could tell me.

